# Line-ending and text handling.
#
# Without this file the working copy follows each machine's `core.autocrlf`,
# which is why `*.sh` was being checked out with CRLF on Windows: a shell script
# with CRLF can fail on Linux, WSL or Git Bash (`bad interpreter`). Shell scripts
# therefore keep LF everywhere; everything else is normalised to LF in the
# repository and checked out with the platform's ending.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf

# Windows batch files are the opposite case: cmd.exe needs CRLF.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Everything else: store LF, let the checkout follow the platform.
* text=auto
